  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.",
  "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $-\\operatorname{csc}\u2061(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer.",
